{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>And he came, etc., and behold, standing at, etc.</b> One needs to know what it comes to teach us by saying <b>and behold, standing</b>, etc. And according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Yalkut Shimoni 109) who said that Eliezer recognized in Laban's running toward evil, so he pronounced the explicit Name and suspended the camels in the air over the spring — and this is what it says, <b>the camels at the spring</b>, while he stood over the camels, as it is written, ״and behold, standing,״ etc. — and from a high place Laban called out to him, and this is what it says, ״Come in, blessed of HaShem.״ And after he came down from the high place, he spoke to him, ״Why do you stand outside?״ And with this it is not difficult why he prefaced by saying ״Come,״ etc., and afterward said ״Why do you stand,״ etc., for it is evident that he should say ״Why do you stand״ and afterward say ״Come,״ etc.:" "<b>Further,</b> it may intend along this line: <b>and behold, standing</b>, etc. — that even though the man asked of Rebekah, ״Is there in your father's house a place,״ etc., and she said, ״Also straw,״ etc., ״also place,״ etc., nevertheless for this reason he did not turn after her to go to her father's house, but stood in his place at the spring." "And His saying <b>come in, blessed</b>, etc., and afterward His saying ״why,״ etc. — the verse intends to tell him that what he invited him to come to him was not to lodge him in his house, but only that he not stand outside; but his need was always for himself, for ״the eyes of the wicked shall fail״ (Job 11:20)."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:43:13.590362757Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}